<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haldimand Proclamation</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1784">1784</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Haldimand Grant</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Haldimand Proclamation was a decree issued on 25 October 1784 by Frederick Haldimand, governor of the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>, granting the Six Nations of the Iroquois a tract of land six miles deep on each side of the Grand River in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>. It rewarded those who had fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a> and lost their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homelands</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. Most of the tract was later sold or alienated, a loss still disputed today.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haldimand-proclamation.jpg" alt="Haldimand Proclamation" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Creator:Thomas Ridout · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thomas_Ridout_map_of_Grand_River_Indian_Lands,_1821.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
